{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And if men strive.</b> Meaning, each one intends to kill the other, and the blow falls upon the woman. In this, the verse states all the aspects of the laws discussed in the matter." "<b>And they strike, etc.</b> It uses plural language, to say that if both of them fell upon her and struck her, they divide [the payment] among them in thirds, as Rambam wrote in chapter 1 of Hilkhot Ḥovel u-Mazzik." "<b>A woman with child.</b> Meaning, one who is present there with their knowledge. But if she came and stood within their bounds and they struck her without knowledge — they are exempt from everything, by force of the ruling written in the Yerushalmi, which Rambam ruled in chapter 1 of Hilkhot Ḥovel u-Mazzik, and this is his language: if one was sleeping and another came and lay down beside him, the one sleeping is exempt, and so is the one who was harmed [exempt from liability], for this is called an accident (ones)." "<b>As the husband of the woman shall lay upon him.</b> Meaning, since he is the one who takes [compensation for] the value of the offspring, he is the one who has jurisdiction over this punishment, to take from him what is fitting for him."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {2 "The quote from Rambam (Hilkhot Ḥovel u-Mazzik 1:12) uses the phrase גם מהנזק, which Or HaChaim appears to render as גם מהניזק; translated literally as 'and so is the one who was harmed [exempt].' Thelogic of the passage is that the sleeper bears no liability, and the analogy applies to the men who struck the woman unknowingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:05.084051079Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}